      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND AIM",
          "content": "The aim of the study was to assess the impact of individualized nutritional intervention according to DASH principles (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) on the nutritional status, blood pressure, and selected biochemical parameters of obese /overweight patients with primary arterial hypertension."
        },
        {
          "Label": "M ETHODS",
          "content": "131 participants were randomized to the DASH intervention group (DIG; n=69, 33 males) or the control group (CG; n=62, 32 males). A three month nutritional intervention was carried out in the DIG, while the control group received only standard recommendations. Body weight, height, waist and hip circumference, body composition, blood pressure, serum glucose, insulin and leptin concentrations were measured at the baseline and after the intervention."
        },
        {
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "64 (92,8%) participants in the intervention and 62 (100%) in the control group completed the study. A significant decrease in body mass, systolic and diastolic blood pressure, body fat content, fasting glucose, insulin and leptin concentrations along with respective anthropometric and biochemical indices was observed in the DIG group (P < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The DASH dietary intervention provides significant benefits to overweight/obese patients with primary hypertension."
        }
      ]},
